  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period of \nbreezy to strong NE winds through early this week with \nstrongest winds along the coast, especially south of Cape \nHatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, combined with \nupcoming King Tide cycle lead to additional minor coastal \nimpacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore continue in the 7-9 foot\nrange and will remain in this range today, and then slowly \nsubside through the coming week, but long period swells will\ncontinue to cause issues for vulnerable areas in conjunction\nwith the building King Tides. For ocean-side: high tide is \nare 1930edt tonight and between 0730-0800edt MON. Soundside \nhigh tides lag from 2 to 3 hours behind the oceanside tides. The\nNational Parks Service and NCDOT have reported substantial \nbeach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along with \nsignificant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due \nto hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed \nfrom the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nCoastal flooding will be likely well into this week. Even with \nwinds diminishing by mid week, \"sunny day\" coastal flooding \nacross much of the tidal regions of ENC around times of high \ntide will continue through at least mid week, then slowly\nimprove as the King Tides ease later in the week. The areas \nmost susceptible to street flooding will be low lying areas \nalong the Neuse and Bay rivers as well as Downeast Carteret \nCounty to North Topsail Beach which has already been seen during\nthe last two morning's high tides. Some flooding occured with\nthis morning's high tide in Beaufort County, so have added them\nto the advisories with water levels only getting higher through\nmidweek.  \n\nThere is still potential that the headlines for TUE and WED may\nneed to be upgraded to coastal flood warnings. Will wait for \nthis decision after we see what MON morning's high tide brings.\nIn addition, if seas increase any further than currently \nforecast, a period of coastal flood warnings may be needed for \nparts of the OBX, and will need to monitor trends.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pockets of moisture continue to be squeezed \nbetween the inland high and offshore low, though almost all \nguidance keeps the bulk of precip activity off the coast and \nperhaps skirting OBX. Most areas remain dry today, along with \nseasonably cool temps in the low 70s interior to 60s coast. \nPleasantly cooler and drier air mass will remain with us through\nearly this week, with near climo highs, to slightly below avg \nlows, esp Sun and Mon nights when good radiational cooling yet \nagain sets up with lows well down into the 40s with some 30s \nagain possible TUE morning when the high pressure center is\ncloser to ENC allowing nocturnal winds to fall off harder and\nskies to clear more than the last few nights. Hts/thicknesses \nthen begin to build by mid week, with temps climbing back above \nclimo with highs in the mid 70s, then upper 70s by weeks end. \nLows will remain closer to climo as dry airmass remains with a \nlarge diurnal temp range.",
